From 14ba08e7925fff250284c98598dd2a0e5cfcf835 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DoubleGate <parobek@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 01:26:04 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] [UPSTREAM] analysis: permanent-identity disclosure detector
 (T-OR-054)

Upstream's imsi_requested analyzer watches the request side (the network
asking for an identity, and the stateful request-without-auth sequence).
This adds the response side: whether the UE actually surrendered a
permanent identity, which is the disclosure that matters.

IdentityDisclosureAnalyzer (default off) flags, keying on the message and
the decoded identity type alone (never the digits, per the in-memory
identity contract):
- Identity Response carrying an IMSI -> Medium (the permanent subscriber
  identity was sent in the clear; imsi_requested judges the home-network /
  no-auth context that makes it an attack).
- Identity Response carrying an IMEI/IMEISV -> High (a network rarely needs
  the permanent device identity via the NAS identity procedure).
- Attach Request using an IMSI as its EPS mobile identity instead of a GUTI
  -> Low (what a CSS forces via an identity-wiping reject, but also a
  legitimate first attach with no stored GUTI).

Relies on the T-OR-110 mobile-identity decoding (patch 0012). Off by
default pending per-network calibration: a UE with no stored GUTI discloses
its IMSI on a genuine first attach. Tests cover each type and severity on
both carriers, the GUTI/temporary non-firing cases, and the message never
containing the digits.

